{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Query is now written in "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to make sure the library and your projects are type-safe!"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Things to keep in mind:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Types currently require using TypeScript v4.1 or greater"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Changes to types in this repository are considered "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"non-breaking"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and are usually released as "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"patch"}]},{"type":"text","value":" semver changes (otherwise every type enhancement would be a major version!)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"highly recommended that you lock your react-query package version to a specific patch release and upgrade with the expectation that types may be fixed or upgraded between any release"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The non-type-related public API of React Query still follows semver very strictly."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-inference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Inference"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Types in React Query generally flow through very well so that you don't have to provide type annotations for yourself"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { data } = useQuery({\n  //    ^? const data: number | undefined\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0icALwoM2XHgAUAbSqDkIAEa4qAXQA0cFQEo5APjgAFciGAYAdLVQQANgDd0KgKxmzXgB6ILgw8IA9AH5eIA","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { data } = useQuery({\n  //      ^? const data: string | undefined\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  select: (data) => data.toString(),\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0icALwoM2XHgAUAbSox0IqgF0ANHBUBKOQD44ABXIhgGAHS1UEADYA3dCoCsxw0gwu6EwAXHASUuZhknT2MBAAyjBQwIIA5iaExrwA9Nlw+QUAegD8vEA","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This works best if your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" has a well-defined returned type. Keep in mind that most data fetching libraries return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"any"},{"type":"text","value":" per default, so make sure to extract it to a properly typed function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const fetchGroups = (): Promise<Group[]> =>\n  axios.get('/groups').then((response) => response.data)\n\nconst { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const data: Group[] | undefined","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-narrowing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Narrowing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Query uses a "},{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/typescript-in-5-minutes-func.html#discriminated-unions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"discriminated union type"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the query result, discriminated by the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":" field and the derived status boolean flags. This will allow you to check for e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"success"},{"type":"text","value":" status to make "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" defined:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { data, isSuccess } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})\n\nif (isSuccess) {\n  data\n  //  ^? const data: number\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0ANHGCoAysgYN0qVETgBeFBmy48ACgDaVGGphUAurMMBKbQD44ABXIh56AHS1UEADYAbuiGAKx2dry8wCRwhvJKKmqoDgi8cBlwElK8APS5GQB6APy8hLxAA","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typing-the-error-field","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typing the error field"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The type for error defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":". This is in line with what TypeScript gives you per default in a catch clauses (see "},{"type":"link","href":"https://devblogs.microsoft.com/typescript/announcing-typescript-4-4/#use-unknown-catch-variables","children":[{"type":"text","value":"useUnknownInCatchVariables"}]},{"type":"text","value":"). The safest way to work with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" would be to perform a runtime check; another way would be to explicitly define types for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: unknown\n\nif (error instanceof Error) {\n  error\n  // ^? const error: Error\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { error } = useQuery<Group[], Error>(['groups'], fetchGroups)\n//      ^? const error: Error | null","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFCiSw4dAB7AIqUuUpURY1Nx68YeMOjgBxcsjBwAvIjjAAJgC44AO2QgARriK9eDCOdTwS6GAwAWmiNon6ABQAlGYAClLAGAA8vtoA2gC6AHx6qbLiAHQA5h6BVAD02Vpg8sGZMF7o5oG0qJAuarqpdQ0YmUZ0MHTBDjxOLvBIuORQRHooGNi4eLElSQA0cACiUKPJgfFUxX6lVIlL7p4+Jai9PAUFcNc3AHoA-LxAA","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"further-reading","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Further Reading"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For tips and tricks around type inference, have a look at "},{"type":"link","href":"../community/tkdodos-blog#6-react-query-and-typescript","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Query and TypeScript"}]},{"type":"text","value":" from\nthe Community Resources. To find out how to get the best possible type-safety, you can read "},{"type":"link","href":"../community/tkdodos-blog#19-type-safe-react-query","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type-safe React Query"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"type-inference","text":"Type Inference","level":2},{"id":"type-narrowing","text":"Type Narrowing","level":2},{"id":"typing-the-error-field","text":"Typing the error field","level":2},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further Reading","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nReact Query is now written in **TypeScript** to make sure the library and your projects are type-safe!\n\nThings to keep in mind:\n\n- Types currently require using TypeScript v4.1 or greater\n- Changes to types in this repository are considered **non-breaking** and are usually released as **patch** semver changes (otherwise every type enhancement would be a major version!).\n- It is **highly recommended that you lock your react-query package version to a specific patch release and upgrade with the expectation that types may be fixed or upgraded between any release**\n- The non-type-related public API of React Query still follows semver very strictly.\n\n## Type Inference\n\nTypes in React Query generally flow through very well so that you don't have to provide type annotations for yourself\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference1'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({\n  //    ^? const data: number | undefined\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0icALwoM2XHgAUAbSqDkIAEa4qAXQA0cFQEo5APjgAFciGAYAdLVQQANgDd0KgKxmzXgB6ILgw8IA9AH5eIA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference1'\n[//]: # 'TypeInference2'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({\n  //      ^? const data: string | undefined\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  select: (data) => data.toString(),\n})\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0icALwoM2XHgAUAbSox0IqgF0ANHBUBKOQD44ABXIhgGAHS1UEADYA3dCoCsxw0gwu6EwAXHASUuZhknT2MBAAyjBQwIIA5iaExrwA9Nlw+QUAegD8vEA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference2'\n\nThis works best if your `queryFn` has a well-defined returned type. Keep in mind that most data fetching libraries return `any` per default, so make sure to extract it to a properly typed function:\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference3'\n\n```tsx\nconst fetchGroups = (): Promise<Group[]> =>\n  axios.get('/groups').then((response) => response.data)\n\nconst { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const data: Group[] | undefined\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference3'\n\n## Type Narrowing\n\nReact Query uses a [discriminated union type](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/typescript-in-5-minutes-func.html#discriminated-unions) for the query result, discriminated by the `status` field and the derived status boolean flags. This will allow you to check for e.g. `success` status to make `data` defined:\n\n[//]: # 'TypeNarrowing'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data, isSuccess } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})\n\nif (isSuccess) {\n  data\n  //  ^? const data: number\n}\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0ANHGCoAysgYN0qVETgBeFBmy48ACgDaVGGphUAurMMBKbQD44ABXIh56AHS1UEADYAbuiGAKx2dry8wCRwhvJKKmqoDgi8cBlwElK8APS5GQB6APy8hLxAA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeNarrowing'\n\n## Typing the error field\n\nThe type for error defaults to `unknown`. This is in line with what TypeScript gives you per default in a catch clauses (see [useUnknownInCatchVariables](https://devblogs.microsoft.com/typescript/announcing-typescript-4-4/#use-unknown-catch-variables)). The safest way to work with `error` would be to perform a runtime check; another way would be to explicitly define types for `data` and `error`:\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError'\n\n```tsx\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: unknown\n\nif (error instanceof Error) {\n  error\n  // ^? const error: Error\n}\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#)\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError'\n[//]: # 'TypingError2'\n\n```tsx\nconst { error } = useQuery<Group[], Error>(['groups'], fetchGroups)\n//      ^? const error: Error | null\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFCiSw4dAB7AIqUuUpURY1Nx68YeMOjgBxcsjBwAvIjjAAJgC44AO2QgARriK9eDCOdTwS6GAwAWmiNon6ABQAlGYAClLAGAA8vtoA2gC6AHx6qbLiAHQA5h6BVAD02Vpg8sGZMF7o5oG0qJAuarqpdQ0YmUZ0MHTBDjxOLvBIuORQRHooGNi4eLElSQA0cACiUKPJgfFUxX6lVIlL7p4+Jai9PAUFcNc3AHoA-LxAA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError2'\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n\n## Further Reading\n\nFor tips and tricks around type inference, have a look at [React Query and TypeScript](../community/tkdodos-blog#6-react-query-and-typescript) from\nthe Community Resources. To find out how to get the best possible type-safety, you can read [Type-safe React Query](../community/tkdodos-blog#19-type-safe-react-query).\n\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: typescript\ntitle: TypeScript\n---\n\nReact Query is now written in **TypeScript** to make sure the library and your projects are type-safe!\n\nThings to keep in mind:\n\n- Types currently require using TypeScript v4.1 or greater\n- Changes to types in this repository are considered **non-breaking** and are usually released as **patch** semver changes (otherwise every type enhancement would be a major version!).\n- It is **highly recommended that you lock your react-query package version to a specific patch release and upgrade with the expectation that types may be fixed or upgraded between any release**\n- The non-type-related public API of React Query still follows semver very strictly.\n\n## Type Inference\n\nTypes in React Query generally flow through very well so that you don't have to provide type annotations for yourself\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference1'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({\n  //    ^? const data: number | undefined\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0icALwoM2XHgAUAbSqDkIAEa4qAXQA0cFQEo5APjgAFciGAYAdLVQQANgDd0KgKxmzXgB6ILgw8IA9AH5eIA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference1'\n[//]: # 'TypeInference2'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({\n  //      ^? const data: string | undefined\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  select: (data) => data.toString(),\n})\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0icALwoM2XHgAUAbSox0IqgF0ANHBUBKOQD44ABXIhgGAHS1UEADYA3dCoCsxw0gwu6EwAXHASUuZhknT2MBAAyjBQwIIA5iaExrwA9Nlw+QUAegD8vEA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference2'\n\nThis works best if your `queryFn` has a well-defined returned type. Keep in mind that most data fetching libraries return `any` per default, so make sure to extract it to a properly typed function:\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference3'\n\n```tsx\nconst fetchGroups = (): Promise<Group[]> =>\n  axios.get('/groups').then((response) => response.data)\n\nconst { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const data: Group[] | undefined\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference3'\n\n## Type Narrowing\n\nReact Query uses a [discriminated union type](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/typescript-in-5-minutes-func.html#discriminated-unions) for the query result, discriminated by the `status` field and the derived status boolean flags. This will allow you to check for e.g. `success` status to make `data` defined:\n\n[//]: # 'TypeNarrowing'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data, isSuccess } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})\n\nif (isSuccess) {\n  data\n  //  ^? const data: number\n}\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0ANHGCoAysgYN0qVETgBeFBmy48ACgDaVGGphUAurMMBKbQD44ABXIh56AHS1UEADYAbuiGAKx2dry8wCRwhvJKKmqoDgi8cBlwElK8APS5GQB6APy8hLxAA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeNarrowing'\n\n## Typing the error field\n\nThe type for error defaults to `unknown`. This is in line with what TypeScript gives you per default in a catch clauses (see [useUnknownInCatchVariables](https://devblogs.microsoft.com/typescript/announcing-typescript-4-4/#use-unknown-catch-variables)). The safest way to work with `error` would be to perform a runtime check; another way would be to explicitly define types for `data` and `error`:\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError'\n\n```tsx\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: unknown\n\nif (error instanceof Error) {\n  error\n  // ^? const error: Error\n}\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#)\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError'\n[//]: # 'TypingError2'\n\n```tsx\nconst { error } = useQuery<Group[], Error>(['groups'], fetchGroups)\n//      ^? const error: Error | null\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFCiSw4dAB7AIqUuUpURY1Nx68YeMOjgBxcsjBwAvIjjAAJgC44AO2QgARriK9eDCOdTwS6GAwAWmiNon6ABQAlGYAClLAGAA8vtoA2gC6AHx6qbLiAHQA5h6BVAD02Vpg8sGZMF7o5oG0qJAuarqpdQ0YmUZ0MHTBDjxOLvBIuORQRHooGNi4eLElSQA0cACiUKPJgfFUxX6lVIlL7p4+Jai9PAUFcNc3AHoA-LxAA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError2'\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n\n## Further Reading\n\nFor tips and tricks around type inference, have a look at [React Query and TypeScript](../community/tkdodos-blog#6-react-query-and-typescript) from\nthe Community Resources. To find out how to get the best possible type-safety, you can read [Type-safe React Query](../community/tkdodos-blog#19-type-safe-react-query).\n\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n"}
